# General Project Guidelines

## Project Structure
- The project follows a modular structure with clear separation of concerns
- Main application code resides in the `app` directory
- Documentation is stored in the `docs` directory
- Tests are organized in the `tests` directory mirroring the app structure

## Version Control
- Use meaningful commit messages following conventional commits format
- Branch naming: `feature/`, `bugfix/`, `enhancement/`, `docs/` prefixes
- Create pull requests for all significant changes
- Keep commits focused and atomic

## Code Style
- Follow PEP 8 style guide for Python code
- Use type hints for all function parameters and return values
- Maximum line length: 100 characters
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Document all public functions and classes

## Dependencies
- Keep dependencies up to date
- Document all requirements in `requirements.txt`
- Pin dependency versions for stability
- Use virtual environments for development

## Documentation
- Keep documentation up to date with code changes
- Document all major features in `docs/`
- Include examples and use cases

# Cursor Coding Rules

## Modular Design
- Always adhere to modular design ideals: keep code organized, separated by concern, and easy to extend or maintain.

## Code Change Policy
- When asked to make changes, do not remove or replace any existing code unless explicitly instructed to do so. Add new features or logic in a way that preserves all working code.

## Changelog Requirement
- For every code change, append a description of the step to `logs/change.log` as a changelog entry. Each entry should briefly describe what was changed and why.  Maintain changelog in `logs/CHANGELOG.md`.  Date/timestamp each entry.
